    "agent_messages": [
      "I added POST /purchases/bulk-reject. It builds, but I haven't run it against a database: credentials aren't available here and the repo has no tests. So none of the reject or concurrency behaviour has actually been run.\n\nThe only file changed is `internal/candidate/approval.go`:\n- **New Action:** I declared `Reject = action.Transition{Operation: \"bulk-reject.v4\", From: \"submitted\", To: \"rejected\", Field: \"rejectedIds\"}`.\n- **Shared handler:** a new `handler(executor, transition)` function calls `Executor.Run` and converts `action.Rejection` into `protocol.Rejection`. Bulk-approve now goes through this function too. It behaves the same as before: it still uses `action.Approve` and the same error mapping.\n- **Routing:** both paths are registered in `protocol.Routes` and served by `protocol.HTTP`. That means request body, identity, size limit and Idempotency-Key checks are the same as for bulk-approve.\n\nThe rest of the requirements come from reading the existing `action` kernel and `SCHEMA.sql`, without running them:\n- **Idempotency:** the kernel scopes keys by operation, tenant, actor and key. It returns stored results on replay and returns 409 if the same key comes with a different set of IDs.\n- **All-or-nothing:** if any target isn't eligible, the kernel returns 409 and rolls back the whole transaction, so nothing changes.\n- **ActionRequest:** the row is written in the same transaction as the status change. If that write fails, the request returns 503 and the transaction is rolled back, so the same key can be retried later.\n- **Concurrency:** rows are locked with `FOR UPDATE` and must still be `status = 'submitted'`. So when two requests hit the same purchase, including an approve racing a reject, only one succeeds and the other gets 409. Overlapping batches under different keys work the same way.\n- **Re-rejecting:** rejected or approved purchases can't be decided again, because both the kernel and the v4 `purchase_decide` RLS policy require `submitted`.\n\n`go build ./...`, `go vet` and `gofmt` are clean, and `go test ./...` passes only because there are no test files. I didn't add tests, because tests are protected and real checks would need the private database."
    ],
